### What is a Monolith?

One deployable artifact, one process. The problem it solves is simplicity: one codebase, one build, one deploy, in-process calls, easy transactions. Right default for small teams and early products.

```java
// Monolith: all features in one deployable
package com.bank;

@SpringBootApplication
public class BankApplication {
    // Transfers, Accounts, Fraud, Notifications all here
}
```

### What is a Modular Monolith?

Still one deployable, but internally split into modules with enforced boundaries. Solves maintainability and future-proofing. Recommended starting point before microservices.

```java
// Modular Monolith: clear module interfaces
package com.bank.transfers;
public interface TransferModule {
    Transfer execute(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount);
}

package com.bank.accounts;
public interface AccountModule {
    BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);
}
// Modules interact through interfaces, not by reaching into internals
```

### What are Microservices?

Many small, independently deployable services, each owning its own data. Solves independent deployment, scaling, and team autonomy. Cost: distributed complexity.

### How do Layered, Microkernel, and Hexagonal fit?

These are code-organization styles that live INSIDE a deployable, not alternatives to a deployment choice.

| Style | Axis | Problem Solved |
|---|---|---|
| Monolith | Deployment | Simplicity |
| Modular Monolith | Deployment | Maintainability |
| Microservices | Deployment | Independence |
| Layered | Code organization | Separation of concerns |
| Microkernel | Code organization | Extensibility |
| Hexagonal | Code organization | Testability, tech independence |
| Space-Based | Data architecture | Extreme load |
| Peer-to-Peer | Network topology | Decentralization |

### When should you use each?

```java
// WRONG: reaching for microservices because they are trendy
// A 3-person team building an MVP does not need 15 services.

// CORRECT: match the style to the problem
if (smallTeam && earlyProduct) use(MONOLITH);
else if (multipleteams && needIndependence) use(MICROSERVICES);
// Apply hexagonal INSIDE each service for testability
```

### What is Space-Based architecture?

Keeps state in an in-memory data grid replicated across nodes, removing the database bottleneck under extreme bursty load. Used for trading systems and flash sales.

### What is Peer-to-Peer?

No central coordinator; every node is both client and server. Solves decentralization (blockchain, file sharing, mesh networks).

### Are these styles mutually exclusive?

No. A real banking platform is often microservices (deployment), where each service is internally hexagonal (code organization), one service is a microkernel product engine, a hot trading path is space-based, and an inter-bank settlement is peer-to-peer.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The classic mistake is adopting microservices for resume value when a modular monolith would serve better.
- Start simple, split only when a real need arises.
- Choosing a style is choosing which problem to prioritize.
- You combine styles across deployment and code-organization dimensions.
